  "intro": {
    "title": "It's Week 44 of 2025 and frameworks sometimes hurt my head",
    "content": "<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\"><img src=\"https://image.mux.com/YIuXXAizsuY01OC02OgZ6tfbZwECNIBP9Bnmo5xdNZRZo/animated.gif?width=600&start=13\" alt=\"Near Future Laboratory Work Kit of Design Fiction in Action\" style=\"margin: 20px 0;\" /></p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">I now realize that I'm an anti-frameworks kinda guy.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">Maybe that's too strong.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">I don't *hate frameworks. I just have trouble with them.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">Like..actually cognitively. They hurt my brain meat a bit.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">When I use them, I begin to see a kind of feeling of surety build up — a field of slightly satisfied confidence that leaves out the unexpected and potential for the excess that lives outside of that structure, and that confidence in the effectiveness of a framework.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">I found this old video that some students made back in the day showing the Work Kit of Design Fiction in action and that got me thinking about the power of open-ended, unframed exploration. No frames, just terrain to explore.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">The Work Kit was meant to be simple and playful.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">What struck me watching it again is how little structure it actually needs.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">The cards give you just enough of a prompt to get going — a hint of order — and then the rest depends on imagination, improvisation, and a willingness to make meaning out of the mess.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">Over the years I’ve learned that some people need structure and rules to begin. That’s fine. But the deeper skill — the one that matters most in creative and strategic work — is learning to move comfortably in open-ended space.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">Too much structure, too much reliance on frameworks and analytics, dulls that muscle. It narrows one's field of view.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">You stop noticing emergence. You stop being surprised.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">When you over-index on structure, you start mistaking the map for the terrain.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">We build elaborate frameworks to protect ourselves from ambiguity — and in doing so, we lose sensitivity to the unexpected, to the weak signals, to the moments of real breakthrough.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">That’s what the Work Kit was designed to help with: to practice the art of embracing ambiguity in a playful, generative way.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\">Think of it as kettlebells or crossfit for the imagination — a way to strengthen your ability to contend with the unknown.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333;\"><a href=\"https://nearfuturelaboratory.com/blog/2025/10/no-rules-of-play-design-fiction-work-kit/\">No Rules of Play</a>.</p>",
    "viewOnlineLink": "https://nearfuturelaboratory.com/newsletters/2025/w44-y25"
  },
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "sponsor",
      "title": "CODE BASE",
      "textColor": "#202020",
      "sponsorLabel": "TOOLING",
      "sponsorLink": "https://github.com/bleeckerj/Files2Book",
      "items": [
        {
          "title": "Out of the Cloud and into a Book\n",
          "subtitle": "I turned my digital life into printed books",
          "description": "<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: Domine, serif !important; font-size: 17px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\">You've heard it before — the web is getting more and more enshittified. Platforms rise and fall, ToS change, content disappears, and all the while your digital life scrolls away into oblivion.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: Domine, serif !important; font-size: 17px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\">This was largely motivated by my desire to preserve my 8+ years of work on <a href='https://omata.com' style=\"font-weight: normal !important; text-decoration: underline !important; color: #EE3BBB !important\">OMATA</a>, mostly solo, and the digital traces of that work that existed in Slack channels, Instagram posts, and various other digital artifacts.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: Domine, serif !important; font-size: 17px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\">What would it take to turn all that digital stuff into something tangible, something real, something you could hold in your hands and put on your bookshelf?</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: Domine, serif !important; font-size: 17px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\">Thus I spent a bewildering, engrossing and occasionally frustrating amount of time (I suppose I could check the commit log, but I'm afraid) building a set of mostly Python tools that can basically transform most digital things into books.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: Domine, serif !important; font-size: 17px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\">Point it at a directory hierarchy of files, or a Slack or Instagram export, and out comes a print-ready PDF book. I even pointed it at my 7 years of weeknotes that I heaved out of a gigantic Markdown file and made a book out of that. Actually, that became two 600 page volumes.</p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: Domine, serif !important; font-size: 17px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\"><span style='background-color: #f1f3e4;  font-weight: 300;'>P.S. That's gif there is every instagram post I made for OMATA from 2015 - 2025, printed into a book. (Videos became a grid of thumbnails.)</span></p>\n<p style=\"margin: 0 0 16px 0; padding: 0;    ; font-family: Domine, serif !important; font-size: 17px !important; line-height: 1.2 !important; color: #111111 !important; text-align: left !important\"><span style='background-color: #f1f3e4; font-weight: 300;'>P.P.S. I ran it on IG Stories too, which was..interesting..Over 6000 pages to represent all of that. T'would make a mighty tome..or Gagosian gallery installation.</span></p>",
          "image": "https://imagedelivery.net/gaLGizR3kCgx5yRLtiRIOw/c20f188f-f3e2-4e47-419e-0fb0482bb800/w=300",
          "readMoreText": "Fork this ▶",
          "link": "https://github.com/bleeckerj/Files2Book"
        }
      ],
      "containerStyles": {
        "backgroundColor": null,
        "padding": "15px 20px",
        "borderRadius": "0px"
      }
    },
